A||||6||BAM-BAM||Lowering spring question......Oreif?||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||07-22-2000||07:25 PM||||  I just installed my new lowering springs (Eibachs) and am a little worried about the fact that the Bearing plate doesn't tighten. I recall from earlier posts that this may be common. Orief, didn't you have this problem? What was the final conclusion? TIA for all your help.<P>  Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> <P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||FIEROCIOUS||07-23-2000||02:03 PM||||It was I with that problem! I posted about a year ago about it. It is normal I even called Chris at Eibach he also said it is normal. I never had a problem with it. However, I only used the Eibachs for a month or so. I eventualy switched to Ryan motorsports adjustables. In day to day traffic you will (never) get the spring out of the perch. I tried! So don't worry about it. Also don't use your bump stop! it will ride like crap! It's the black dust-boot thingy. If you have any other questions feel free to ask.||||reg||1
Z||000002||webbee||07-23-2000||03:48 PM||||Fierocious why did you go to the Ryan moto's?<BR>Can you compare them to the Eibacs, like ride, hadeling, how far they lower. Details MAN! TIA||||reg||5
Z||000003||Oreif||07-23-2000||10:44 PM||||I already sent Bam-Bam the response via Email, But this is for everyone else. The Eibach springs will be loose with no weight on the car. There are 2 ways to correct this if you go with the Eibachs. First, Nylon wire ties and some drilled out holes will keep the springs from shifting. I have heard but haven't seen it done was using Chevy Citation Struts instead of Fiero Struts. The shaft is suppose to be shorter. If someone out there works for an auto parts place how about getting a Fiero strut and a Citation strut and measure the difference for us?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||07-24-2000||04:19 AM||||The Ryan coil overs adjust + or- 3 inches The ride is better than the Eibach.<P>However, I had a rear sway bar and in combination with the progressive nature of the Eibachs it worked well on smooth sufaces but not on real world surfaces it caused a chatter on hard corners. I like my car to handle well as I race it in SCCA events. That fact and the ability to change spring rates easily, made my decission.<P>The ryane springs come out of their perches too but not as bad  (at 2" lowered.)<P>The Eibachs in my opinion are designed to work without a rear sway bar. I tried it without the sway bar it DID handle substantualy better. <P>The ryane springs are linear rate springs and work better with a sway bar.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||07-24-2000||11:35 PM||||  Thanks everyone for all of your input. I am going to go to a parts house and check out the citation thing myself. If it's a go, I may just see if KYB supplies the citation stut. I believe the physics of the KYB delima, however I am not completely comfortable with even the smallest probability of my springs coming unpearched.<P> Thanks again, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||12
Z||000006||lowCG||07-26-2000||01:17 AM||||The Citation strut housings will lower your car a little with your existing springs,a little more with lowering springs;the perch is just welded on lower,so there will be even more empty space when your car is on the jack with the Eibach springs.<BR>The reason I used the Citation units was that they have a removable cartridge,allowing me to use some Koni rebuildable/adjustable/re-valvable struts I had laying around.||||reg||1
